arXiv:2509.12857v1 [eess.SP] 16 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-Play
<br class="ltx_break">Diffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yi Zhang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>This research was supported by the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation program (grant No. 101000967) and by the Israel Science Foundation (grant No. 536/22). This work is also supported by Manya Igel Centre for Biomedical Engineering and Signal Processing.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rui Guo
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: Rui Guo (rui.guo@weizmann.ac.il).</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yonina C. Eldar
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a posterior sampling algorithm for the problem of estimating multiple independent source signals from their noisy superposition. The proposed algorithm is a combination of Gibbs sampling method and plug-and-play (PnP) diffusion priors. Unlike most existing diffusion-model-based approaches for signal separation, our method allows source priors to be learned separately and flexibly combined without retraining. Moreover, under the assumption of perfect diffusion model training, the proposed method provably produces samples from the posterior distribution. Experiments on the task of heartbeat extraction from mixtures with synthetic motion artifacts demonstrate the superior performance of our method over existing approaches.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
signal separation, source separation, diffusion, Gibbs sampling, plug-and-play

</div><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>Faculty of Math and Computer Science, Weizmann Institute of Science, Rehovot, Israel</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the problem of recovering <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> independent source signals <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> from their noisy superposition:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\boldsymbol{v}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\boldsymbol{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the observational noise <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma_{v}^{2}\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma_{v}^{2}\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of sources.
This formulation arises in diverse applications, such as speech separation in the “cocktail party problem” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, clutter suppression in ultrasound imaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and vital sign monitoring using radar <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Classical model-driven approaches often rely on simplified priors on the sources, such as periodicity or narrowband structure, leading to decomposition methods like empirical mode decomposition (EMD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>) or variational mode decomposition (VMD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>). Blind separation techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, such as independent component analysis (ICA) and nonnegative matrix factorization (NMF), exploit statistical independence of the sources or nonnegativity assumptions, but are mainly tailored for multi-channel observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of deep learning, data-driven approaches have become dominant. Earlier methods typically adopt a discriminative paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, training a neural network to map noisy mixtures to separated sources. While effective, such approaches usually require retraining when the number or prior distributions of sources change, and they only yield point estimates, offering no uncertainty quantification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, generative models have emerged as powerful priors for inverse problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
Diffusion models, as the state-of-the-art in this family, allow sampling from the posterior distribution <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}_{K}|\boldsymbol{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}_{K}|\boldsymbol{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling approximation of the MMSE estimator <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{s}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}_{K}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{y}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{s}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}_{K}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{y}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> by posterior sample average and facilitating uncertainty quantification. However, most existing diffusion-model-based approaches for signal separation either train diffusion models to directly sample from the posterior distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, requiring retraining when the number or prior distributions of sources change, or lack theoretical guarantees even if the training of diffusion models is perfect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a posterior sampling algorithm for Bayesian signal separation, based on Gibbs sampling and plug-and-play (PnP) diffusion priors. Our main contributions are threefold:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a modular posterior sampling framework that alternates Gibbs updates of individual sources using their corresponding diffusion models, allowing source priors to be learned separately and flexibly combined without retraining.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish a consistency guarantee for the proposed algorithm, showing that it produces exact posterior samples under perfect diffusion model training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test the proposed algorithm on the task of extracting heartbeat signals from mixtures with synthetic motion artifacts, demonstrating its superior performance over existing approaches.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation:</span> For <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq j</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{i:j}\triangleq(\boldsymbol{s}_{i},\boldsymbol{s}_{i+1},\dots,\boldsymbol{s}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{i:j}\triangleq(\boldsymbol{s}_{i},\boldsymbol{s}_{i+1},\dots,\boldsymbol{s}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). For a random variable <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote a realization of it by <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background on Diffusion Models</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Stochastic differential equation (SDE)-based diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> have been widely used for modeling complex probability distributions. Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the data distribution. By gradually injecting noise through a diffusion process, <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be transformed into a Gaussian noise distribution. A diffusion model then performs sampling by simulating the corresponding reverse-time diffusion process, which converts Gaussian noise samples back into samples from <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The forward diffusion process is a Markov process <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with initial state <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> and transition kernel given by the following SDE<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            In the general case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, the forward process can be written as <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\boldsymbol{x}_{t}=f(\boldsymbol{x},t)dt+g(t)d\boldsymbol{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\boldsymbol{x}_{t}=f(\boldsymbol{x},t)dt+g(t)d\boldsymbol{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we set <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\boldsymbol{x},t)\equiv\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\boldsymbol{x},t)\equiv\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity, which already covers many state-of-the-art diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\boldsymbol{x}_{t}=g(t)d\boldsymbol{w}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\boldsymbol{x}_{t}=g(t)d\boldsymbol{w}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Wiener process and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the noise injection rate at time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Integrating (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) yields</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}=\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\int_{0}^{t}g(s)d\boldsymbol{w}_{s}\sim p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma(t)\boldsymbol{n}}=:p_{\sigma(t)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}=\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\int_{0}^{t}g(s)d\boldsymbol{w}_{s}\sim p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma(t)\boldsymbol{n}}=:p_{\sigma(t)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(t)\triangleq\sqrt{\int_{0}^{t}g^{2}(s)ds}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(t)\triangleq\sqrt{\int_{0}^{t}g^{2}(s)ds}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard deviation of the injected noise. Notice that the marginal distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is much larger than the standard deviation of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may approximate <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> by a Gaussian distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the marginal distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{0}}=p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{0}}=p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a Gaussian distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The forward process (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) admits a reverse-time counterpart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The reverse process is a Markov process <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with initial state being <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{T}\overset{d}{=}\boldsymbol{x}_{T}\sim p_{\sigma(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{T}\overset{d}{=}\boldsymbol{x}_{T}\sim p_{\sigma(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> and transition kernel given by the reverse-time SDE</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}={-g^{2}(t)\nabla_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}}\log p_{\sigma(t)}(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t})}dt+g(t)d\bar{\boldsymbol{w}}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}={-g^{2}(t)\nabla_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}}\log p_{\sigma(t)}(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t})}dt+g(t)d\bar{\boldsymbol{w}}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{w}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{w}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Wiener process independent of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any positive integer <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and any finite set of times <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t_{1}&lt;t_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;t_{N}\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t_{1}&lt;t_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;t_{N}\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>, it is known that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{1}},\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{2}},\cdots,\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{N}}\right)\overset{d}{=}\left({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{1}},{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{2}},\cdots,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{N}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑑</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{1}},\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{2}},\cdots,\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{N}}\right)\overset{d}{=}\left({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{1}},{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{2}},\cdots,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{N}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">i.e., if one observes <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at a sequence of time points, the joint statistics are indistinguishable from those of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus running <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> backward in time faithfully reproduces the law of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which explains why it is referred to as the time reversal of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, according to (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the marginal distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{T}}=p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{T}}\approx\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{T}}=p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{T}}\approx\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}}=p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{0}}=p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}}=p_{\boldsymbol{x}_{0}}=p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that sampling from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{T}}\approx\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{T}}\approx\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is straightforward, simulating (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> yields samples from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Implementation of Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), the function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{\sigma(t)}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{\sigma(t)}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (termed <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">score function</span>) has no closed-form expression and needs to be approximated by a neural network. By Tweedie’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, for any <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\forall\boldsymbol{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{d})\;\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}}\log p_{\sigma}(\boldsymbol{z})=\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{z}\right]-\boldsymbol{z}}{\sigma^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\forall\boldsymbol{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{d})\;\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}}\log p_{\sigma}(\boldsymbol{z})=\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{z}\right]-\boldsymbol{z}}{\sigma^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{z}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{z}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is the MMSE estimator of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> given the noisy observation <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus it can be approximated by a denoising neural network <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{z}\right]\approx D_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{z};\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\,\middle|\,\boldsymbol{x}_{0}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}=\boldsymbol{z}\right]\approx D_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{z};\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}}\log p_{\sigma}(\boldsymbol{z})\approx\frac{D_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{z};\sigma)-\boldsymbol{z}}{\sigma^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{z}}\log p_{\sigma}(\boldsymbol{z})\approx\frac{D_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{z};\sigma)-\boldsymbol{z}}{\sigma^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The reverse-time diffusion process (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) can then be simulated by numerical integration. A common choice is the Euler–Maruyama method, which discretizes <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> into steps <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=t_{0}&gt;t_{1}&gt;\cdots&gt;t_{M}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=t_{0}&gt;t_{1}&gt;\cdots&gt;t_{M}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{0}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{0}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}(T)\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can simulate (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) by updating</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i+1}}\leftarrow\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i}}+g^{2}(t_{i})\frac{D_{\theta}(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i}};\sigma(t_{i}))-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i}}}{\sigma^{2}(t_{i})}h_{i}+g(t_{i})\sqrt{h_{i}}\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i+1}}\leftarrow\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i}}+g^{2}(t_{i})\frac{D_{\theta}(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i}};\sigma(t_{i}))-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{i}}}{\sigma^{2}(t_{i})}h_{i}+g(t_{i})\sqrt{h_{i}}\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\triangleq t_{i}-t_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\triangleq t_{i}-t_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, the score function in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is replaced by (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.2 Implementation of Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Then <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as an approximate sample from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, the denoising network and numerical solver together constitute a diffusion model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method and Analysis</h2>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Gibbs sampling method</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
            
            
           initial value <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{2},\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{3},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{K}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{2},\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{3},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{K}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, observation <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
            
            
           number of iterations <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0b" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0b.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
            
            
           posterior sample <math id="alg1.l0b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{1},\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{2},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{K}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{1},\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{2},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{K}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             draw <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{k}\sim{p}_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{k}\sim{p}_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given observation <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we aim to generate samples from the posterior distribution <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}|\boldsymbol{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}|\boldsymbol{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be further used to approximate the MMSE estimator or other statistics of interest. A standard approach is Gibbs sampling (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), which iteratively updates one component of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}</annotation></semantics></math> at a time by sampling from its conditional distribution given the others. However, for general prior distributions <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>), the required conditional samplers are intractable, posing a key challenge for practical implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that with a diffusion model for sampling each prior <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional sampling in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be realized by partially simulating the reverse diffusion process (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). This yields our Diffusion-within-Gibbs (DiG) algorithm for Bayesian signal separation. Next we detail the derivation of the DiG algorithm, and prove that, under perfect training, DiG produces exact posterior samples.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>An Alternative Formulation of the Conditional PDF</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first provide an alternative formulation for the conditional pdf <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}\mid\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}\mid\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which will be useful in the following derivation. By (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and Bayes’ theorem, we have</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\times p_{\boldsymbol{y}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}\middle|\;\cdot\;,\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.335em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\times p_{\boldsymbol{y}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}\middle|\;\cdot\;,\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}(\cdot)\;e^{-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{v}^{2}}\left\lVert\boldsymbol{r}^{(i)}_{k}-\;\cdot\;\right\rVert^{2}_{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.113em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}(\cdot)\;e^{-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{v}^{2}}\left\lVert\boldsymbol{r}^{(i)}_{k}-\;\cdot\;\right\rVert^{2}_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we define <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{r}^{(i)}_{k}\triangleq\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{r}^{(i)}_{k}\triangleq\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for brevity; (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1 An Alternative Formulation of the Conditional PDF ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) follows from independence of sources and (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{n}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{n}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\boldsymbol{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> be independent of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the following auxiliary pdf:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|{\boldsymbol{r}}^{(i)}_{k}\right)\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|{\boldsymbol{r}}^{(i)}_{k}\right)\propto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}\left(\cdot\right)\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}\left({\boldsymbol{r}}^{(i)}_{k}\middle|\;\cdot\;\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.335em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}\left(\cdot\right)\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}\left({\boldsymbol{r}}^{(i)}_{k}\middle|\;\cdot\;\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}\left(\cdot\right)\;e^{-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{v}^{2}}\left\lVert{\boldsymbol{r}}^{(i)}_{k}-\;\cdot\;\right\rVert^{2}_{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.113em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}\left(\cdot\right)\;e^{-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{v}^{2}}\left\lVert{\boldsymbol{r}}^{(i)}_{k}-\;\cdot\;\right\rVert^{2}_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then by comparing (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.1 An Alternative Formulation of the Conditional PDF ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.1 An Alternative Formulation of the Conditional PDF ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), we can derive</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)\\
=&amp;\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum\nolimits_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}\right).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.504em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.504em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)\\
=&amp;\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum\nolimits_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Hence to implement Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it suffices to sample from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}\mid\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}\mid\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma_{v}\boldsymbol{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next we show this conditional sampling is equivalent to simulating a partial reverse-time diffusion process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose that we have access to a dataset of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can generate new samples from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> by simulating a reverse-time diffusion process <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, as introduced in Sec. <a href="#S2" title="2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Its initial state is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,T}\sim p^{(k)}_{\sigma(T)}\triangleq p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma(T)\boldsymbol{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≜</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,T}\sim p^{(k)}_{\sigma(T)}\triangleq p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma(T)\boldsymbol{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the transition kernel is governed by the reverse-time SDE</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}=-g^{2}(t)\,\nabla_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}}\log p^{(k)}_{\sigma(t)}\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)\,dt+g(t)\,d\bar{\boldsymbol{w}}_{k,t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}=-g^{2}(t)\,\nabla_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}}\log p^{(k)}_{\sigma(t)}\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)\,dt+g(t)\,d\bar{\boldsymbol{w}}_{k,t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(k)}_{\sigma}\triangleq p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≜</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(k)}_{\sigma}\triangleq p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma\boldsymbol{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recalling (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Forward and Reverse-Time Diffusion Processes ‣ 2 Background on Diffusion Models ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), one can verify that</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}|\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}|\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma(t)\boldsymbol{n}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{s}_{k}+\sigma(t)\boldsymbol{n}}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then by (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1 An Alternative Formulation of the Conditional PDF ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), if there exists <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{v}\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{v}\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(t_{v})=\sigma_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(t_{v})=\sigma_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}|\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}},\boldsymbol{s}_{1:k-1}^{(i)},\boldsymbol{s}_{k+1:K}^{(i-1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\mid\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t_{v}}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum\nolimits_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo rspace="0.113em">(</mo><mo rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;p_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}\mid\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t_{v}}}\left(\;\cdot\;\middle|\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum\nolimits_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Thus by setting <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t_{v}}\leftarrow\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum\nolimits_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t_{v}}\leftarrow\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum\nolimits_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and simulating (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, the resultant <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be regarded as a realization of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Applying this result to Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> yields the proposed <span id="S3.SS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion-within-Gibbs</span> (DiG) algorithm; see Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Remarkably, the DiG algorithm only requires pre-trained diffusion models for the priors of individual source signals. Hence, unlike existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> that require retraining when the number of sources or the prior distributions change, DiG can flexibly plug pre-trained diffusion models into various signal separation problems.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Diffusion-within-Gibbs (DiG) algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             initial value <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{2},\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{3},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{K}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{2},\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{3},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(0)}_{K}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, observation <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             number of iterations <math id="alg2.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, noise level <math id="alg2.l0a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l0b" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0b.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             train <math id="alg2.l0b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> diffusion models for simulating the reverse-time SDE (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) for all <math id="alg2.l0b.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\left\{1,2,\dots,K\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\left\{1,2,\dots,K\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l0c" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0c.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             posterior sample <math id="alg2.l0c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{1},\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{2},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{K}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{1},\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{2},\cdots,\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{K}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             find <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{v}\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{v}\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(t_{v})=\sigma_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(t_{v})=\sigma_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t_{v}}\leftarrow\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,t_{v}}\leftarrow\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}-\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{j}-\sum_{j=k+1}^{K}\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               obtain <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}</annotation></semantics></math> by simulating (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) from <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{k}\leftarrow\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{k}\leftarrow\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{k,0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg2.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Consistency with an Ideal Diffusion Simulator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we establish the consistency of DiG, namely that under an ideal diffusion simulator, it produces exact posterior samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that for all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has a strictly positive density with respect to the Lebesgue measure, and that the simulation of (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is exact.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Here, “exact simulation of (</span><a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a><span id="footnote2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">)” means that, in the diffusion model used to simulate (</span><a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a><span id="footnote2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">), the denoising network perfectly learns the MMSE estimator and the discretization step size in the numerical solver vanishes.</span></span></span></span> Then the joint distribution of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{1:K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}^{(N)}_{1:K}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> converges in total variation distance to the true posterior <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}\mid\boldsymbol{y}=\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}\mid\boldsymbol{y}=\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">When (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is simulated exactly, the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th iteration of Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.2 Conditional Sampling Through Diffusion ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is equivalent to that of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Method and Analysis ‣ Bayesian Signal Separation via Plug-and-PlayDiffusion-Within-Gibbs Sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Hence, the Markov transition kernel from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{1:K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}^{(i-1)}_{1:K}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{1:K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}^{(i)}_{1:K}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, is reversible with respect to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}\mid\boldsymbol{y}=\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{1:K}\mid\boldsymbol{y}=\hat{\boldsymbol{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since for each <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{s}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive everywhere, one can verify that <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible and aperiodic. Thus we can derive the result from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Thm. 1 and Cor. 1]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, several other diffusion-model-based posterior sampling methods have also been shown to achieve consistency with ideal diffusion simulators for more general inverse problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. However, such guarantees are typically asymptotic, requiring either the number of samples maintained in each iteration to approach infinity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> or certain annealing parameter to vanish arbitrarily slowly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, thus providing weaker guarantees than the proposed DiG algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
